              "title": "Task 1",
              "instructions": "Read the passage and choose the correct answers. /6x2=12 points/",
              "text": "(1) Every school morning, sixteen-year-old Temuulen's alarm rings at half past six, and every morning it feels like the middle of the night. His parents used to think he was simply lazy. \"Go to bed earlier,\" they said. But when he tried, he lay awake until midnight anyway.\n\n(2) Sleep scientists say that Temuulen is completely normal. During the teenage years, the body's internal clock shifts, so teenagers naturally start to feel sleepy later in the evening than children or adults do. \"Asking a teenager to fall asleep at nine is like asking an adult to fall asleep at seven in the evening,\" explains one sleep doctor. \"It is not about laziness; it is biology.\"\n\n(3) To understand how much teenagers really sleep, researchers in many countries have asked students to wear small devices on their wrists that record when they are asleep. They have compared these records with the students' school timetables and their marks.\n\n(4) The connection is striking. Most teenagers need between eight and ten hours of sleep a night, but many get fewer than seven on school days. Students who regularly sleep too little find it harder to concentrate, remember information and control their moods.\n\n(5) Some schools have responded by starting lessons later in the morning, and many of them report that students arrive more alert and miss fewer classes. Others argue that a later start makes life difficult for working parents and for after-school sports. Still, experts agree on one simple rule: a regular bedtime and fewer screens at night help everyone sleep better.",
